Prompt for Flutter App
Update the existing Flutter Firebase IoT application to complete Firebase Web configuration.

The application already contains:
- Firebase Authentication using FirebaseAuth
- AuthGate widget
- Login/Register screen
- Existing IoT dashboard
- Firebase Realtime Database integration
- firebase_options.dart created for Web configuration

Now integrate the Firebase Web App configuration.

Requirements:

1. Update Firebase initialization:

- Ensure Firebase.initializeApp() works correctly on:
  - Android
  - Web (Chrome)

- Use DefaultFirebaseOptions.currentPlatform.

Example:

await Firebase.initializeApp(
  options: DefaultFirebaseOptions.currentPlatform,
);


2. Update firebase_options.dart:

Create a complete Firebase configuration file.

The configuration must support:

Web:
- apiKey
- appId
- messagingSenderId
- projectId
- authDomain
- databaseURL
- storageBucket

Android:
- Keep existing Android Firebase configuration.


3. Add Firebase Web configuration values:

Use these values:

FIREBASE_API_KEY:
[PASTE YOUR API KEY HERE]

FIREBASE_APP_ID:
[PASTE YOUR APP ID HERE]

FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID:
[PASTE YOUR MESSAGING SENDER ID HERE]

FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID:
[PASTE YOUR PROJECT ID HERE]

FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN:
[PASTE YOUR AUTH DOMAIN HERE]

FIREBASE_DATABASE_URL:
[PASTE YOUR REALTIME DATABASE URL HERE]

FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET:
[PASTE YOUR STORAGE BUCKET HERE]


4. Verify Firebase Authentication:

Check that:

- Email/password login works on Chrome
- User registration creates Firebase Authentication users
- Existing logged-in users automatically open dashboard
- Logout returns to authentication screen


5. Verify Realtime Database:

The database structure must remain:

users/
   {userUID}/
        devices/
             esp32_01/
                  temperature
                  relay1
                  relay2


All Flutter database listeners and writes must use:

FirebaseAuth.instance.currentUser!.uid


Example:

Before:

devices/esp32_01/temperature


After:

users/{userUID}/devices/esp32_01/temperature


6. Add error handling:

If Firebase initialization fails:

- Do not show a blank Chrome page
- Display a clear startup error message
- Show missing Firebase configuration information


7. Test the application:

Run:

flutter analyze

flutter test

flutter build web


Confirm that:

- Chrome opens correctly
- Login screen appears when no user is logged in
- Dashboard appears after login
- Database reads and writes work correctly


Do not remove:
- BMP280 temperature monitoring
- Relay 1 control
- Relay 2 control
- Existing dashboard UI design

Only add Firebase Web configuration and connect it with the existing authentication system.


Prompt for ESP32
Update the existing ESP32 Firebase IoT firmware.

The existing firmware already contains:

- WiFi connection
- Firebase Realtime Database connection
- BMP280 temperature sensor reading
- Relay 1 control
- Relay 2 control
- Existing Firebase read/write operations

The Flutter application has now been updated with Firebase Authentication.

The Firebase Realtime Database structure has changed.

Old structure:

devices/
    esp32_01/
        temperature
        relay1
        relay2


New structure:

users/
    USER_UID/
        devices/
            esp32_01/
                name
                sensors/
                    temperature
                relays/
                    relay1/
                        state
                    relay2/
                        state


Update the ESP32 firmware according to this new structure.

Requirements:

1. Add permanent user assignment.

Create a constant:

const char* USER_UID = "VVL9RSKbFDPkEpbDhOVOIjKAIjl2";


This ESP32 device belongs permanently to this Firebase user.

The ESP32 does not need Firebase Authentication login.

It should use this UID to build all database paths.


2. Update temperature upload path:

Replace the old temperature path:

devices/esp32_01/temperature


with:

users/{USER_UID}/devices/esp32_01/sensors/temperature


The BMP280 temperature reading should continue working exactly as before.


3. Update relay control paths:

Relay 1:

Read state from:

users/{USER_UID}/devices/esp32_01/relays/relay1/state


Relay 2:

Read state from:

users/{USER_UID}/devices/esp32_01/relays/relay2/state


When Flutter changes relay state:

ESP32 should receive the updated value and control the GPIO output.


4. Keep existing functionality:

Do not remove:

- WiFi connection code
- Firebase initialization
- BMP280 sensor code
- Relay GPIO configuration
- Existing timing logic


Only update Firebase database paths.


5. Add serial debugging:

Print Firebase paths during startup.

Example:

User UID:
xxxxxxxx

Temperature path:
users/xxxxxxxx/devices/esp32_01/sensors/temperature

Relay paths:
users/xxxxxxxx/devices/esp32_01/relays/relay1/state
users/xxxxxxxx/devices/esp32_01/relays/relay2/state


6. Verify Firebase operations:

Temperature:

ESP32 → Firebase

users/{UID}/devices/esp32_01/sensors/temperature


Relay:

Flutter → Firebase → ESP32

users/{UID}/devices/esp32_01/relays/relay/state


7. Keep the code compatible with the current Firebase Arduino library used in the project.

After modification:

- Compile successfully
- Upload successfully
- Confirm temperature updates in Firebase
- Confirm Flutter relay buttons control ESP32 relays

